===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|rhizoma}}

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|lang1=grc|ῥίζα|t1=root|-ome}}. As philosophical metaphor used by {{w|Gilles Deleuze}} and {{w|Félix Guattari}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɹaɪzoʊm/}}
{{rfap|en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|botany}} A [[horizontal]], [[underground]] [[stem]] of some [[plant]]s that sends out [[root]]s and [[shoot]]s ([[scion]]s) from its [[node]]s.
#: {{syn|en|rootstalk}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=George Bacon Wood|authorlink=George Bacon Wood|title=A Treatise on Therapeutics, and Pharmacology, Or Materia Medica|year=1868|publisher=J. B. Lippincott & Company|location=Philadelphia|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=OT1HAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA432&dq=%22rootstalk%22&hl=en&cd=7&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22rootstalk%22&f=false|page=432|passage=All these species are climbing, briery plants, having long slender roots, which proceed in all directions from a common rootstalk or '''rhizome'''.}}
# {{lb|en|philosophy|critical theory}} A so-called “image of thought” that [[apprehend]]s [[multiplicity|multiplicities]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Ronald Bogue|title=Deleuze and Guattari|year=1989|publisher=Psychology Press|page=107|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=YeiHUoRVINEC&pg=PA107&dq=%22rhizome%22&hl=en&cd=20&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22rhizome%22&f=false|isbn=978-0-415-02443-3|passage=The corpus of Kafka's writing, they argue, is ‘a '''rhizome''', a burrow’ (K 7)—an  uncentered and meandering growth like crab grass, a complex, aleatory network of pathways like a rabbit warren. A '''rhizome''', as Deleuze and Guattari explain in ''Rhizome: an Introduction'' (1976), is the antithesis of a root-tree structure, or ‘arborescence’, the structural model which has dominated Western thought from Porphyrian trees, to Linnaean taxonomies, to Chomskyan sentence diagrams.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|editors=Amanda Spink; Michael Zimmer|title=Web Search: Multidisciplinary Perspectives|chapter=Reconsidering the Rhizome|author=A. Hess|year=2008|publisher=Springer Science & Business Media|page=35|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=nopJzzYeNREC&pg=PA35&dq=%22rhizome%22&hl=en&cd=45&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22rhizome%22&f=false|isbn=978-3-540-75829-7|editor2=|passage=Critical theorists have often drawn from Deleuze and Guattari's notion of the '''rhizome''' when discussing the potential of the Internet. While the Internet may structurally appear as a '''rhizome''', its day-to-day usage by millions via search engines precludes experiencing the random interconnectedness and potential democratizing function.}}

